## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about scanning a 128-bit value with fixed, branch-reduced steps and no loops. It exercises bitwise practice and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-width binary search over bit positions
- Handling x==0 gracefully
- Portability across 128-bit types

## Code Example
```c
// Conceptual outline (branch-free version would use masks)
int msbIndex128(uint128 x) {
    if (x == 0) return -1; // illustrative; actual solution avoids branches
    int idx = 0;
    if (x >> 64) { x >>= 64; idx += 64; }
    if (x >> 32) { x >>= 32; idx += 32; }
    if (x >> 16) { x >>= 16; idx += 16; }
    if (x >> 8)  { x >>= 8;  idx += 8; }
    if (x >> 4)  { x >>= 4;  idx += 4; }
    if (x >> 2)  { x >>= 2;  idx += 2; }
    if (x >> 1)  { idx += 1; }
    return idx;
}
```

